  "answer_text": "## Only Fools and Horses \u2013 Del Falls Through the Bar\n\n### The Episode: **\"Yuppy Love\"** (Series 6, Episode 1)\n\n\"Yuppy Love\" was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, with a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### The Scene\n\nA barman lifts up the section of the bar that Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down, leaving a gap \u2014 all behind Del's back without him noticing. Del then tells Trigger to \"play it nice and cool\" before leaning back and falling straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nTrigger looks around, baffled, wondering where Del has gone. Del gets up and tells Trigger they are leaving.\n\n---\n\n### Behind the Scenes\n\nThe scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar, except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall all the way over.\n\nSullivan originally wanted a slip and stumble, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 falling sideways without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot to get right, as it was difficult not to look where he was falling; luckily, Jason had experience doing falls in the theatre.\n\nInterestingly, a rehearsal script that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to be there on the day and writer John Sullivan decided to include him. They got Trigger's blue suit and put him in the scene, which was mostly **improvised**.\n\n---\n\n### Legacy\n\nOften known as the \"Del falls through the bar episode,\" it is regarded as one of the **classic comedy sketches of all time** and was voted the **number one comedy moment of all time** in a UK poll.\n\nDel falling through the bar also inspired a later scene 25 years later in the *Sport Relief* mini-episode *Beckham in Peckham* (2014), in which David Beckham leans on a portable rack \u2014 which has been moved \u2014 and falls to the floor.",
